const AUDIO_REVIEW_DATA = [{"id":1,"number":1,"title":"נ״ט בר נ״ט — Foundations","src":"/audio/2026-summer/nat-bar-nat-foundations.m4a","transcript":[{"start":0.5,"end":2.26,"text":"Short and sweet, khazar number one."},{"start":2.42,"end":5.56,"text":"We began learning this semester that there's a concept of tamke iker,"},{"start":5.64,"end":11.96,"text":"that the flavor which is absorbed into the walls of a keli, of a utensil, is considered like the food itself."},{"start":12.08,"end":17.48,"text":"So if milk is absorbed into the walls of a pot, and then meat would be cooked in that pot,"},{"start":17.7,"end":20.46,"text":"we would consider as if the milk and the meat was cooked together,"},{"start":20.62,"end":24.92,"text":"and assuming that the pot was a ben yomo, that it was used in the past 24 hours for milk,"},{"start":25.06,"end":28.62,"text":"the meat that was cooked inside the pot would be asr da araysa,"},{"start":28.62,"end":31.0,"text":"Just like if the actual milk and meat was cooked together."},{"start":31.28,"end":38.52,"text":"We then learned about the concept of Nat Barnat, which is in a situation where a par of food is cooked inside of a pot,"},{"start":38.56,"end":42.34,"text":"which has some sort of flavor absorbed into it, whether that is meat or dairy."},{"start":42.5,"end":50.3,"text":"The par of food, which is now cooked in this pot, in this utensil, absorbs flavor from the walls of the pot."},{"start":50.4,"end":55.66,"text":"However, through that absorption, through the cooking process, the flavor is degraded and it is somewhat weakened."},{"start":55.66,"end":60.42,"text":"And the power of food is now called a Nat Barnat, a נותן טעם בר נותן טעם."},{"start":60.64,"end":68.8,"text":"All opinions agree that once you have a Nat Barnat, whether of meat or dairy, it would be allowed to be eaten either before or after the other type,"},{"start":68.9,"end":73.06,"text":"and it would also be allowed to be served with the utensils of the other type."},{"start":73.22,"end":78.94,"text":"So, if a person would eat a Nat Barnat of meat, they can definitely eat dairy immediately afterwards,"},{"start":78.94,"end":87.94,"text":"And it could also have that of a not-bar-not that was cooked in a meat pot can be served with dairy utensils."},{"start":88.02,"end":96.04,"text":"A common case and a common question that comes up is when a person wants to mix a not-bar-not with the other type."},{"start":96.24,"end":103.9,"text":"So a classic case that we will use would be a person is cooking pasta, par of pasta, in a fillet-shaped pot, in a meat pot,"},{"start":103.98,"end":107.28,"text":"and they then want to use that pasta with cheese."},{"start":107.28,"end":110.16,"text":"So it's going to depend a lot on the status of the pot."},{"start":110.3,"end":116.2,"text":"We'll begin by discussing the case of the benyomo fleshig pot, if the pot was used in the past 24 hours to cook meat."},{"start":116.36,"end":125.04,"text":"So, if the person is right now in a lechadchila situation, they are pre-doing anything,"},{"start":125.3,"end":133.16,"text":"they plan on cooking this part of pasta in a meat benyomo pot, and their intention is to serve it with dairy."},{"start":133.16,"end":135.16,"text":"So according to the Ramah, this is forbidden."},{"start":135.34,"end":137.34,"text":"L'chachila, this is asr, a person cannot do this."},{"start":137.5,"end":143.48,"text":"According to the Shulchan Aruch, it's a macholkes, there are those who said the Shulchan Aruch holds it's forbidden, while others, or Rav Ovadya says it's permitted."},{"start":143.76,"end":153.02,"text":"And in a case where it's, let's say, a b'dievah, they already have cooked the pasta in the fleshyk pot, and now they want to air dairy, so this is a macholkes in the Shulchan Aruch and the Ramah."},{"start":153.66,"end":162.52,"text":"The Shulchan Aruch holds that since the pasta is already a not-bar-not of fleshyks, it can be now mixed with the cheese."},{"start":162.52,"end":167.9,"text":"However, the Ramah holds, נותן לחכילה, we cannot mix a knot by a knot of fleisheks with cheese."},{"start":168.14,"end":173.04,"text":"A third scenario would be if, let's say, a person had already cooked the pasta in the fleishek benyomah pot,"},{"start":173.2,"end":176.28,"text":"and they already had mixed it with cheese, and they come to the rabbi and say,"},{"start":176.36,"end":178.72,"text":"what's the status of my pasta with cheese?"},{"start":179.04,"end":184.48,"text":"Everyone agrees that Shulchan Aruch and Ramah both agree that the food is b'dievet, post facto, permitted."},{"start":184.76,"end":188.0,"text":"Another scenario would be if a person now has an eno benyomah fleishek pot."},{"start":188.08,"end":190.66,"text":"This pot is meat, but has not been used in the past 24 hours."},{"start":190.66,"end":192.98,"text":"Okay, so then we have the same three scenarios."},{"start":193.16,"end":199.12,"text":"If a person would want to cook pasta in this Einu Ben Yom HaFleshik pot in order to mix it with cheese,"},{"start":199.32,"end":200.84,"text":"the Shulchan Aruch holds it is permitted."},{"start":200.96,"end":203.86,"text":"Everyone agrees that in the Shulchan Aruch's opinion, this is permitted."},{"start":204.16,"end":209.76,"text":"In the Ramaz camp, that's a Machokas, the Gra, the Vilna Goan said it's also permitted,"},{"start":209.86,"end":216.24,"text":"the Ramah Gris is permitted, while the Chochmas Adam holds that a person should not do this if they have a par pot available."},{"start":216.24,"end":223.22,"text":"But if they do not, then even the Chochmas Adam says a person can use the Eno Ben Yom HaFleshik pot to cook par pasta and mix it with cheese."},{"start":223.44,"end":229.92,"text":"Let's say a situation was that a person already had cooked the pasta in the Eno Ben Yom HaFleshik pot, and now they want to mix it with dairy."},{"start":230.14,"end":232.46,"text":"So then everybody agrees that's permitted as well."},{"start":232.5,"end":241.34,"text":"For sure, if a person had cooked the pasta in the Eno Ben Yom HaFleshik pot and had already mixed it with cheese, of course, all opinions Shulchan Aruch and Rama would agree that it is permitted."},{"start":241.34,"end":259.0,"text":"We mentioned that a very important point about Nat Bar Nat is that Nat Bar Nat only applies to Basar V'chalav because the whole understanding is that when the meat or the dairy flavor inside the pot gets transferred to the par of food, it gets degraded and it's not anymore have its full potency that can become Basar V'chalav."},{"start":259.0,"end":280.52,"text":"However, if let's say an utensil was used to cook something forbidden, and then a part of kosher food was cooked in that forbidden utensil, the flavor does not get degraded. No matter how many times the flavor gets transferred, the flavor is already forbidden, and all the food that is cooked in that utensil is forbidden. And if that food then passes on its flavor to something else, it would also be forbidden."},{"start":280.52,"end":288.0,"text":"We lastly mentioned that if you find food with a DE, a dairy equipment, on the label, that means that the food is a not-bar-not of dairy equipment."},{"start":288.3,"end":294.02,"text":"There are some products marked with a D, which are actually parv, but they're marked D for other considerations."},{"start":294.24,"end":303.92,"text":"In general, a person should never rely on the label of a food, and we should always call the cautious organization and find out if something is truly dairy or is it dairy equipment."}]},{"id":2,"number":2,"title":"נ״ט בר נ״ט — Applications & דבר חריף","src":"/audio/2026-summer/nat-bar-nat-applications-and-davar-charif.m4a","transcript":[{"start":0.46,"end":1.86,"text":"Short and sweet, Hazara number two."},{"start":2.1,"end":20.52,"text":"It is mutter lechachila, it is permissible to initially cook a par of food in a fleshik pot, in a meat pot, in order to serve it on a dairy dish, even while the food is hot, and that would go vice versa as well to the other situation where a person wants to cook something par in a dairy pot and serve it on a meat dish."},{"start":20.7,"end":26.0,"text":"It's even permissible to use a dairy pot to heat up par of food which is cooked in a meat pot and vice versa."},{"start":26.62,"end":31.68,"text":"It's also even permitted to mix two power foods which were cooked in two different pots."},{"start":31.82,"end":36.52,"text":"You can have pasta that was cooked in a flasheek pot and vegetables that was cooked in a milheek pot."},{"start":36.62,"end":43.04,"text":"Even if they're both benyomo, these two power foods which are no longer in the pots that they were cooked in can be mixed together l'chachila."},{"start":43.14,"end":50.44,"text":"We discussed a situation where a person would fry a dovacharif, a sharp food, such as peppers or even onions, we discussed the possibility."},{"start":50.7,"end":55.44,"text":"If a person would fry this dovacharif, this sharp food, in a meat or dairy pan."},{"start":56.14,"end":70.38,"text":"So what would be the status of the Delver Charev? Because it has that sharpness. In a situation where the pan is a benyomo, of meat or dairy, everybody agrees, the Shulchan Aruch and Rama both agree that this food is either meat or dairy, whatever the pot was, and if it was mixed with the other type, it would be forbidden."},{"start":70.38,"end":94.52,"text":"If the pot or the pan was Ein Oben Yomo, it has not been used in 24 hours, that's where we have a Machlokas, the Shulchan Aruch holds that the food is still considered Parv, even though it's sharp, because the pan is Ein Oben Yomo, while the Rama holds that, no, since the food is sharp, it does, it revives the flavor that's inside of the keli, inside of the utensil, and it is considered either meat or dairy, whatever the pan was, and if it's mixed with the other type, it would be forbidden."},{"start":94.52,"end":100.24,"text":"We then had a discussion of what situations would qualify as a not-bar-not."},{"start":100.36,"end":110.54,"text":"So in a situation where a food, either meat or dairy, was cooked in a pot, and then cleaned out, and then a par of food was subsequently cooked in that pot,"},{"start":110.72,"end":117.34,"text":"that is a situation where all post-kim all agree that the second food is considered a not-bar-not,"},{"start":117.4,"end":123.72,"text":"because the flavor went from food to the pot, and then from the pot to another food, and since there was a pot in the middle,"},{"start":123.72,"end":131.76,"text":"The flavor was decreased, it was diminished by being inside the pot, and the par of food, the third food, sorry, the second food is now considered a not-branat."},{"start":131.88,"end":144.6,"text":"Where we had a machlokas is two other situations where there was not a pot in the middle, either the flavor went from one food to another food to a pot, or from one food to another food to another food, that was where we had a machlokas."},{"start":144.6,"end":153.46,"text":"In the different opinions, there are those who held that that is not considered a not-branat because there is no pot in the middle, there are those who held that it is a not-branat because at least there is a pot somewhere."},{"start":153.72,"end":164.76,"text":"And there are those who held that even if there's no pot at all, you can still be considered a Nat Bar Nat because the flavor ultimately does get passed on from one food to the next to the next, which is two steps away from the original food."},{"start":164.9,"end":180.84,"text":"We then discussed the Nat Bar Nat Bishas Bishel, which was in a situation where a food is now going to transfer its flavor into a pot, and then the pot will give its flavor to another food, but it will all happen simultaneously."},{"start":180.84,"end":185.98,"text":"Simultaneously, it will all take place while the pot is on the fire."},{"start":186.1,"end":191.1,"text":"So one example we gave is, let's say, milk splashes on the outside of a pot, and inside the pot there is pasta."},{"start":191.38,"end":196.96,"text":"So since, although the milk hits the pot and then goes from the pot to the water or to the pasta inside of the pot,"},{"start":197.06,"end":203.56,"text":"because it all takes place while the food is on the fire, maybe perhaps we would say that the flavor does not have a chance to get degraded yet,"},{"start":203.56,"end":209.1,"text":"and it goes straight into the food inside of the pot and basically kind of goes right through the pot"},{"start":209.1,"end":213.36,"text":"without stopping it without getting degraded another example was a more common example perhaps"},{"start":213.36,"end":219.5,"text":"is using a sous vide so if you have meat inside of a bag and then the bag inside is of water and"},{"start":219.5,"end":225.06,"text":"the water is inside of a bucket so potentially we would say that the bucket can be considered"},{"start":225.06,"end":229.88,"text":"already a not by not because the flavor goes from the meat to the bag to the bucket or potentially"},{"start":260.3,"end":260.84,"text":"Shas Bishol,"},{"start":274.2,"end":291.54,"text":"Sorry, if a person wanted to know what's the status of the sous vide itself, the actual heating element, this would be the same discussion, because the meat inside the bag can now go through, the flavor of the meat can go through the bag, into the water, and then into the sous vide itself, the actual heating element, and make it flesheks, while according to the Beis Ephraim, it would stay parv."},{"start":291.54,"end":292.38,"text":"What do we do?"},{"start":292.52,"end":300.54,"text":"L'maisah, L'ashkenazim, hold L'chachila, we should follow the Chavas Daas, and L'chachila would consider the suvi, or the bucket that's holding the water, to be fleshicks,"},{"start":300.6,"end":305.12,"text":"while the Sephardim hold L'chachila, we can follow the Beis Ephraim, and it's not a problem."},{"start":305.32,"end":313.0,"text":"Even the Ashkenazim would B'dievet, rely on the Beis Ephraim, and B'dievet, in a situation where, let's say, the suvi was used to cook meat, which is inside of a bag,"},{"start":313.1,"end":320.04,"text":"and then it was used to cook something dairy inside of a bag, B'dievet, the Ashkenazim, would allow that food,"},{"start":320.04,"end":322.3,"text":"And we would rely on the Beis Ephraim B'dievet."},{"start":322.48,"end":327.02,"text":"We mentioned once again that Nat Bar Nat does not apply to things that are inherently prohibited."},{"start":327.2,"end":329.54,"text":"It only applies to Basav HaChalef."},{"start":329.7,"end":336.24,"text":"However, Rav Ovadya did add that in a situation of great need, a person can rely on Te'imas Akim."},{"start":336.32,"end":342.64,"text":"A person can rely on a non-Jew tasting a food which was cooked inside of a utensil, which was inherently forbidden."},{"start":342.84,"end":346.1,"text":"And they could be trusted to say that there's no flavor from the forbidden food."},{"start":346.1,"end":351.34,"text":"The example we gave was by the peas, if a person has canned peas or canned corn, without a heksher."},{"start":351.46,"end":354.88,"text":"So if they're in a real bind, a Sfardi can rely on this and ask a non-Jew,"},{"start":355.3,"end":358.0,"text":"Besir Fituma, without giving any details, but can ask him,"},{"start":358.02,"end":361.54,"text":"do you taste anything like pig or anything other than corn?"},{"start":361.7,"end":365.9,"text":"And to find out if it's actually forbidden, this was something that a Sfardi can rely on."},{"start":365.92,"end":367.96,"text":"However, Ashkenazim do not have this allowance."},{"start":368.2,"end":373.08,"text":"We then discussed a very classic case where a challah was placed on top of a challah pot"},{"start":373.74,"end":398.14,"text":"Chalent was cooking, and obviously there's meat inside of the Chalent, so we were wondering what is the status of this challah? Is the challah parv, or is it fleshek? Well, this would be a case of Nat B'Shash B'Shel, if assuming that the challah was directly contacting the lid, so the meat is giving flavor directly to the lid, and the lid could be giving flavor to the bread, but because it's during cooking, fleshek goes right through, the fleshek flavor goes right through the lid into the challah."},{"start":398.14,"end":400.76,"text":"So this will be dependent on the Malchokas between the Chavas Daas and the Beis Ephraim."},{"start":400.84,"end":407.88,"text":"The Chavas Daas holding that the bread on the lid is now Fleshix, while the Beis Ephraim would say, no, the bread is still Parv, it's considered a Nat Bar Nat of Fleshix."},{"start":408.14,"end":412.62,"text":"At least for the Eved, it would be for Ashkenazim, if a person put cheese on it, it would be okay."},{"start":412.7,"end":416.52,"text":"And if it were into the Mechaber, you'd be able to put cheese on it, the Chachila, according to the Beis Ephraim."},{"start":416.88,"end":418.94,"text":"That's all assuming that the bread is directly on the pot."},{"start":419.14,"end":424.14,"text":"But if a person would put a piece of tinfoil between the Chala and the Chalom Pot, that would be amazing."},{"start":424.14,"end":432.06,"text":"That would be much better because that would be a case of shtei kederos, of two utensils touching each other, which we say flavored snago without rota, without any liquid."},{"start":432.24,"end":439.82,"text":"And therefore, if according to everybody, if a person would wrap the challah in tinfoil, of course, leaving it a little open, maybe to get out of hatamana issues for Shabbos."},{"start":439.82,"end":445.66,"text":"But if a person would put tinfoil between the chalampat and the challah, that would keep the bread par of according to everybody."},{"start":445.66,"end":452.56,"text":"And lastly, we said that a person must always be sure to make sure that canned foods do have a good heksher,"},{"start":452.62,"end":458.94,"text":"because without that, there is a very great concern that they pasteurize the cans with non-kosher water,"},{"start":459.14,"end":469.54,"text":"and that would make the food non-kosher, because it would be a not-ber-not issue with something inherently problematic,"},{"start":469.9,"end":472.26,"text":"which we say is forbidden."}]},{"id":3,"number":3,"title":"Fish & Meat","src":"/audio/2026-summer/fish-and-meat.m4a","transcript":[{"start":0.53,"end":18.71,"text":"Shorts we reviewed number three. We discussed the issue of mixing meat and fish. That we learned is a problem of sakana, is dangerous to mix meat and fish, to the extent that the Shulchan Aruch says that a person has to wash their hands and their mouth, wash out their mouth between eating meat and fish."},{"start":18.71,"end":23.69,"text":"The Rama, on the other hand, says that a person is not required to wash their hands and mouth between meat and fish."},{"start":23.79,"end":26.67,"text":"However, it is preferable to eat and drink something between meat and fish."},{"start":26.77,"end":35.85,"text":"We brought in the opinion of the Magnavram, who says that nowadays the natures of people have changed and it is no longer dangerous to eat meat and fish together."},{"start":36.09,"end":41.79,"text":"We did say that that is a tzirif, that is something that we will rely on in conjunction with other factors to be lenient,"},{"start":41.83,"end":45.81,"text":"but we do not rely on the Magnavram straight and we do not change our practice."},{"start":74.37,"end":75.77,"text":"b'shishim,"},{"start":88.01,"end":92.35,"text":"The suggestion was to boil water in the pot and then spill it out, and that would permit using the pot right away."},{"start":92.53,"end":97.05,"text":"In a situation where a person wants to serve meat and fish on the same table, that was a machokas between the poskim,"},{"start":97.65,"end":104.43,"text":"or Vosner, or Avadya, which said that it would be allowed because the danger of mixing them together is enough to keep people from doing so."},{"start":104.65,"end":109.57,"text":"However, the Kaf HaChaim said that it's forbidden, but again, most poskim we saw said that it would be allowed."},{"start":110.57,"end":115.49,"text":"The fact that it's a sakana that's dangerous would keep people from mixing them together, and it's not the same as meat and milk together."},{"start":115.49,"end":121.95,"text":"We discuss Worcestershire sauce, which contains fish. We discuss the discussion whether or not it should be just a regular OU or an OU fish."},{"start":122.25,"end":129.85,"text":"However, a person should know that if it is not baltabashishim, the OU does hold that they put the fish on the label."},{"start":130.61,"end":134.77,"text":"But even in that situation, a person would be allowed to mix the sauce with more power of ingredients,"},{"start":134.99,"end":141.15,"text":"which will ultimately be mevato and nullify the amount of fish in the sauce to then use it with meat, even l'chachila."}]},{"id":4,"number":4,"title":"Dishes, Sinks & Dishwashers — Flavor Transfer","src":"/audio/2026-summer/dishes-sinks-flavor-transfer.m4a","transcript":[{"start":0.53,"end":28.93,"text":"Short and sweet review number four. We discussed a case where either a person had accidentally put a meat spoon or meat ladle inside of a dairy pot, and inside the pot was yad soledes bo, water or soup, and or similarly, a same case would be if there was a par of soup inside of a pot or hot water inside of a pot, a par of pot, but a meat and dairy spoon were both put in at the same time. Both of these cases are the same halachically."},{"start":28.93,"end":57.73,"text":"Exactly. If one of those utensils, the meat or the dairy utensil, whether it be the spoon or the pot or both spoons, if they have residue on them, so then we're not really dealing with a not-bar-not issue, because we're dealing with residue. Residue is never subject to not-bar-not, it's the actual food. And therefore, a person would have to figure out if there's shishim against the residue, if it's both of a shishim or not. If it is, then we can disregard the residue. However, if there is not 60 times, then we have to deal with the fact that there is an actual meat or dairy inside of this pot."},{"start":57.73,"end":61.69,"text":"We'll focus primarily on a situation where both of them are clean."},{"start":61.77,"end":65.45,"text":"Both the pot and the spoon, or both spoons in the second case, are both clean."},{"start":65.57,"end":68.01,"text":"So if they're both clean, and they're both benyomo,"},{"start":68.23,"end":74.73,"text":"so then we are in a situation where we have two clean utensils of different types"},{"start":74.73,"end":78.23,"text":"mixing inside of a par of water or a par of soup."},{"start":78.43,"end":82.15,"text":"That would be, in a situation like that, it would be a machlokas between the Shulchan Aruch and the Rama."},{"start":82.29,"end":85.63,"text":"The Shulchan Aruch would hold everything is permitted, and this is a case of not-burnat,"},{"start":85.63,"end":90.77,"text":"While the Rama would hold everything is prohibited because the flavors mix together in the liquid."},{"start":90.91,"end":96.37,"text":"In a situation where both of those, Kalim, both utensils were not benyoma, they have not been used in the past 24 hours,"},{"start":96.49,"end":99.89,"text":"and again, they're both clean, everybody agrees that the utensils are permitted."},{"start":100.09,"end":104.33,"text":"However, the Rama says the water should be discarded because water is cheap."},{"start":104.41,"end":105.35,"text":"We can always throw that out."},{"start":105.41,"end":108.21,"text":"If it's a soup, which is something that cannot be discarded so easily,"},{"start":108.41,"end":111.39,"text":"then even the Rama agrees that the food can be eaten, the soup can be eaten."},{"start":111.61,"end":114.35,"text":"Let's say a situation where one was benyoma and one was not benyoma."},{"start":114.49,"end":115.35,"text":"Again, they're both clean."},{"start":115.63,"end":120.29,"text":"In this scenario, again, everyone agrees, the Shulchan Aruch and Rama both agree that everything is permitted."},{"start":120.45,"end":125.43,"text":"If it's a question of the contents of the pot, if it's water, again, the Rama says you should discard the water."},{"start":125.47,"end":127.99,"text":"Water is cheap, but if it's a soup, the soup can be eaten."},{"start":128.11,"end":133.73,"text":"In a situation where we're not putting one type of utensil onto another, but we're dealing with the case of pouring,"},{"start":133.91,"end":141.15,"text":"let's say pouring a par of soup from a meat benyomo pot onto a clean dairy benyomo bowl."},{"start":141.15,"end":151.85,"text":"So then we discuss that in this situation of pouring, where it's lo nisach ha'kiluach, there's an actual stream going from the pot into the bowl, we view it as if the bowl is inside of the pot."},{"start":152.01,"end":156.01,"text":"Well, that's only the bowl is inside the pot, but not the pot is inside of the bowl."},{"start":156.13,"end":162.17,"text":"So in this scenario, for sure the pot is going to be permitted, we view it as completely disconnected from the bowl."},{"start":162.25,"end":164.51,"text":"But the question is, what is the status of the bowl?"},{"start":164.51,"end":173.91,"text":"So the Rama holds that the dairy bowl would be forbidden, it was viewed as if it was actually dipped into the Fleshik pot, however, the liquid will be permitted for various reasons."},{"start":174.11,"end":185.73,"text":"The Shach held that everything is permitted, and the Chochmas Adam said, kind of a midway, if it's not a big loss, then the plate would be forbidden, however, in the case of a loss of money, we would permit the plate like the Shach."},{"start":185.73,"end":194.17,"text":"In a situation where a person inserted a fleshyk utensil into a parv pot and then took it out,"},{"start":194.19,"end":201.05,"text":"and then inserted a dairy utensil into the same pot after the fleshyk utensil was removed,"},{"start":201.35,"end":204.95,"text":"again, in a situation where they're both clean, even if they're both benyomo,"},{"start":205.17,"end":209.27,"text":"everything is permitted because it was done consecutively, they were not in the pot at the same time."},{"start":209.69,"end":213.37,"text":"There are magos so far to say that also in a situation of a klisheni,"},{"start":213.37,"end":237.41,"text":"If let's say you have a pot, which was a pot of water that you cooked up, and then you pour that water into a cold bowl, and then you dumped into that cold bowl, two different types of utensils, a fleshik and a dairy, even if they were dirty, and they had residue on them, since it's in a klisheni, the Rama holds a klisheni does not transfer flavor and everything is permitted, you would rinse them off in cold water, and they would not need to be koshered."},{"start":237.41,"end":259.77,"text":"In a situation also where let's say a person poured hot water from a klirishon, this is a case what we call eroy klirishon, so then the Rama said even if it's, as long as it's eroy klirishon, pouring from a klirishon, that also does not transfer flavor, and even if a person would pour boiling hot water from a klirishon onto dairy, sorry, dirty meat and dairy dishes, everything is still permitted."},{"start":259.77,"end":270.73,"text":"Because of all these factors of klisheni and because of iroi klirishon, the Ramab says if a person finds a clean meat utensil in their dairy utensil drawer, everything is permitted and vice versa."},{"start":270.77,"end":277.45,"text":"Because there are so many factors that are in play and so many spheikos, so many doubts, a person would not have to kasher that utensil."},{"start":277.69,"end":286.91,"text":"Then we discuss a ladle. We discuss a machlokas, whether or not a ladle, when you scoop out from a pot, has a status of a klirishon or does it have a status of a klisheni."},{"start":287.63,"end":292.23,"text":"Everyone agrees though, if it's sat in the pot for long enough to heat up, it does take on the status of a klirishon."},{"start":292.35,"end":295.41,"text":"We also discussed the status of hot water coming out of a sink. That was a big machlokes."},{"start":295.61,"end":301.25,"text":"We saw the Shemir Shabbos Gilchasa held that the hot water coming out of a sink has a status of klirishon, which goes k'dek klipa."},{"start":301.45,"end":309.77,"text":"The Menchas Yitzchak said it has a higher level of eroy klirishon. It's eroy klirishon al ha'esh, because the boiler has the fire on, which is the same as a klirishon as well."},{"start":309.77,"end":313.37,"text":"And the Or Litzion was the most lenient of the opinions."},{"start":313.45,"end":317.57,"text":"He said it's considered E-Rekli Sheni because of the pipes cool off the water,"},{"start":317.63,"end":322.97,"text":"and it would only be E-Rekli Sheni, which cannot transfer flavor and cannot make any problems with dishes."},{"start":323.35,"end":327.47,"text":"We also then would discuss the discussion of sinks, how many sinks a person should have."},{"start":327.57,"end":330.09,"text":"It's always ideal to have separate sinks for meat and dairy."},{"start":330.21,"end":335.03,"text":"If a person only has one sink, so then there was an opinion that said you can still use it for both meat and dairy."},{"start":335.19,"end":339.45,"text":"The Menchas Yitzchak said a person should have a separate rack and a separate bin and should wash out the sink."},{"start":339.77,"end":350.23,"text":"Rav Moshe said that a person only needs a separate rack, but not a separate bin, and Rabelski said you only need a separate bin, well, you don't need a separate rack, but again, the Chachila person should have separate sinks for meat and dairy."}]},{"id":5,"number":5,"title":"Dishes, Sinks & Dishwashers — Practical Rules","src":"/audio/2026-summer/sinks-and-dishwashers-practical-rules.m4a","transcript":[{"start":0.4,"end":15.34,"text":"Short and sweet review number five. We discussed a situation where a person accidentally did the terrible sin of washing a dirty milk utensil in a fleshig meat sink with dirty meat utensils. Terrible, terrible situation."},{"start":15.7,"end":22.14,"text":"Well, we said even though it's so terrible, all the utensils can still be considered permitted for numerous reasons."},{"start":22.32,"end":26.7,"text":"First of all, we know that the water coming out of the sink possibly was not a yad soledes bo."},{"start":26.7,"end":31.28,"text":"Maybe it was below Yat Salat Yisbo, temperature, in which case it would not make any effect on the kelim, on the utensils."},{"start":31.44,"end":36.24,"text":"Also, we mentioned there were shitas who hold, the opinions that hold that maybe a faucet has a status of iri klisheni,"},{"start":36.32,"end":40.16,"text":"which for sure does not transfer any flavor, even if it has iri klisheni on there,"},{"start":40.18,"end":41.96,"text":"Rama holds that that cannot forbid dishes as well."},{"start":42.06,"end":45.52,"text":"We mentioned that most people use soap. Soap works as a po-game."},{"start":45.62,"end":50.26,"text":"It ruins the flavor of the meat or dairy residue and stops any issues from taking place."},{"start":50.5,"end":56.3,"text":"And we have the groundbreaking chiddish of Rav Moshe Feinstein that held that if as long as there was a little bit of cold water on,"},{"start":81.98,"end":83.56,"text":"Shulchan Aruch"},{"start":112.22,"end":113.88,"text":"Shulchan Aruch"},{"start":144.2,"end":145.6,"text":"POSKIM"},{"start":146.3,"end":151.26,"text":"and Dairy, for sure not using them together, but even if you would use the same dishwasher for meat"},{"start":151.26,"end":156.22,"text":"and then dairy dishes one after the next, it's still not something that the Ashkenazi Kiposkin"},{"start":156.22,"end":161.02,"text":"were okay with. First of all, they said that even though soap is used in a dishwasher, it's only"},{"start":161.02,"end":167.08,"text":"added later in the cycle, and first there is a non-soap cycle, which does give flavor. Second of"},{"start":167.08,"end":171.04,"text":"all, there are many times food particles, if they're stuck in the filter or in crevices, and those food"},{"start":171.04,"end":176.04,"text":"particles can make problems in contact, if they're in contact with the wrong type of utensil. And"},{"start":176.04,"end":182.56,"text":"And third of all, since the heating element is inside of the dishwasher, it might actually make the water mamisha klirishon,"},{"start":182.66,"end":187.64,"text":"which would, and you would not get the same allowances of a sink, of maybe being a klisheni."},{"start":187.78,"end":194.12,"text":"However, the Sephardic posts were more lenient, and they allowed using the dishwashers for meat and then dairy,"},{"start":194.18,"end":198.22,"text":"Rav Ovadya even allowed using them together, again, assuming that the initial rinse cycle is cold."},{"start":198.58,"end":201.46,"text":"There are many factors why the Sephardim are more make-all and more lenient."},{"start":201.48,"end":205.7,"text":"First of all, they said even if the dishwasher is a klirishon, it's only iri klirishon,"},{"start":205.7,"end":208.52,"text":"They also are a fan of the soap being po-game."},{"start":208.7,"end":215.86,"text":"And also, third of all, they say it's very unlikely that particles will become in contact with the dishes in a way that will be posing a problem."},{"start":216.0,"end":219.06,"text":"In summary, though, there are many differences between how dishwashers work."},{"start":219.2,"end":220.7,"text":"You have to know how your dishwasher works."},{"start":220.78,"end":222.64,"text":"You have to then ask a Rav for guidance."},{"start":222.76,"end":228.1,"text":"And then before a person uses the same dishwasher for both meat and dairy dishes."}]},{"id":6,"number":6,"title":"Open Foods, Storage & סתם יינם Foundations","src":"/audio/2026-summer/storage-and-stam-yeinam-foundations.m4a","transcript":[{"start":0.59,"end":16.29,"text":"Short and sweet number six. In a bediavad situation where a dairy utensil or dish was washed in a meat dishwasher with other meat dishes, then that dairy utensil or dish would have to be koshered. We do not have the leniencies that we relied on before when we were dealing with a sink."},{"start":16.29,"end":24.57,"text":"In regards to buying coffee at a Starbucks or Dunkin' Donuts, we discussed how it's much more preferable to get a coffee at a Dunkin' Donuts for two reasons."},{"start":24.77,"end":32.69,"text":"First of all, the Dunkin' Donuts chain and franchise is much more kosher conscience, and they prefer kosher products over non-kosher products."},{"start":59.87,"end":62.67,"text":"Yad Tzoletisbo"},{"start":62.69,"end":67.23,"text":"If, however, the meat and dairy was next to the salt and was uncovered,"},{"start":67.47,"end":71.93,"text":"because we are afraid that the meat or the dairy got into the salt,"},{"start":72.01,"end":77.27,"text":"and the salt is now fleshyk or milchik, and salt is something which is used as parv,"},{"start":77.27,"end":78.83,"text":"and can be used with either type."},{"start":79.31,"end":82.55,"text":"B'dievet, if a person already did it, they left the meat or dairy next to the salt, uncovered,"},{"start":82.71,"end":85.55,"text":"or a sugar dish, for example, would also be another example."},{"start":85.77,"end":88.45,"text":"So then, b'dievet, the salt or the sugar is still parv,"},{"start":88.49,"end":91.89,"text":"as long as there is no meat or dairy visible inside of the salt."},{"start":91.89,"end":98.29,"text":"In a situation where a person has meat next to dairy or dairy next to meat, so then again, if it's covered, so then it's totally permitted."},{"start":98.39,"end":101.25,"text":"There's no concern that if it was covered, it got from one to the other."},{"start":101.33,"end":105.83,"text":"In a situation where it was uncovered, the meat or the dairy was uncovered next to the other."},{"start":105.95,"end":108.91,"text":"So that was in Makhloka, so Shulchan Aruch held that everything is still permitted."},{"start":109.15,"end":112.45,"text":"However, the Ramah said, Likha Chila, this is something we should not do."},{"start":113.23,"end":117.59,"text":"And B'di Yeved, we again say that everything is permitted as long as there's no visible mixing."},{"start":117.59,"end":126.39,"text":"So again, the difference between the Shulchan Aruch and the Rama is that Shulchan Aruch says you can Lichad Chila, leave meat next to dairy, uncovered, while the Rama says that one should not do that."},{"start":126.61,"end":135.53,"text":"Okay, in a situation where a person had salt kept in a meat dish, so then the Shulchan Aruch and the Taz both said that it can be used with dairy."},{"start":135.69,"end":140.45,"text":"The Rama said it's praiseworthy not to use the salt that was kept in a meat dish with dairy."},{"start":140.65,"end":146.47,"text":"However, in a situation where it was, it doesn't seem that the Rama would say, but it's a problem."},{"start":156.43,"end":160.33,"text":"We then began the sugya of Stam Yeinam and Yayin Nesech."},{"start":160.41,"end":164.23,"text":"So we explained that there's a very big difference between Yayin Nesech and Stam Yeinam."},{"start":164.29,"end":168.97,"text":"Yayin Nesech is wine which was actually used as a libation for Avodah Zarah."},{"start":169.13,"end":177.95,"text":"If that's the case, the wine is biblically, asr daraisah, prohibited to drink or get benefit from, even in the smallest amounts."},{"start":178.25,"end":182.95,"text":"Stam Yeinam is wine which we do not know to have been used for Avodah Zarah."},{"start":182.95,"end":188.07,"text":"However, it was wine which was either owned or touched by a non-Jew."},{"start":188.15,"end":199.05,"text":"In that scenario, the Chachamim, our Rabbanon, enacted a Rabbinic Gezerah against drinking this wine and from benefiting from the wine."},{"start":199.19,"end":202.49,"text":"There are two different reasons behind each of these prohibitions."},{"start":202.63,"end":207.35,"text":"The prohibition from drinking the wine was in order to prevent intermarriage."},{"start":207.37,"end":212.33,"text":"We don't want us getting too close to non-Jews and Chas HaSholam becoming intermarried with them."},{"start":212.33,"end":219.19,"text":"So the Chachamim forbade drinking this wine which was touched or owned by a non-Jew."},{"start":219.27,"end":226.17,"text":"And the Yisr of Hanna, the prohibition from benefiting it, was from its connection to Yayin Nesech."},{"start":226.29,"end":231.33,"text":"And that's why they also forbade benefiting just like Yayin Nesech has as well."},{"start":231.45,"end":234.93,"text":"And we will explain the next year the different applications nowadays."}]},{"id":7,"number":7,"title":"סתם יינם — Benefit & Commerce","src":"/audio/2026-summer/stam-yeinam-benefit-and-commerce.m4a","transcript":[{"start":0.72,"end":30.64,"text":"Short and sweet number seven. We discussed how the Rishonim have a machlokas, whether or not the Isser of Hanah, the prohibition of benefiting from Stam Yeinam, still applies nowadays. We mentioned that Rashi and the Geonim say that it is permissible to benefit from Stam Yeinam nowadays because non-Jews generally do not libate anymore. They still hold that. Drinking the wine is a problem, but benefiting is permitted. The Rush held that if it's a situation"},{"start":30.64,"end":36.1,"text":"The Rosh Hashanah is a situation where you had wine, which was owned by a Jew, and it was just touched by a non-Jew."},{"start":36.46,"end":39.98,"text":"That's a scenario, the Rosh Hashanah said, we can allow benefit."},{"start":40.32,"end":44.76,"text":"It's only where the, it's a case of Stam Yeinam, where the non-Jew owned the wine himself."},{"start":45.12,"end":47.5,"text":"That's where the Rosh Hashanah said, we still hold that benefit is prohibited."},{"start":47.78,"end":54.96,"text":"The Rambam held that it doesn't matter whether it was touched by a non-Jew or owned by a non-Jew, benefiting from it is prohibited."},{"start":54.96,"end":64.36,"text":"So what comes out, Halach HaMaisah, is that when it comes to drinking wine which was touched or owned by a non-Jew, that is forbidden even b'zban haZeh, there is no machlokas."},{"start":64.68,"end":74.74,"text":"However, when it comes to benefiting from Stam Yeinam, which is referring to wine which was either owned by a non-Jew or wine which was touched by a non-Jew, we had a machlokas between the Shulchan Aruch and the Rama."},{"start":74.74,"end":85.0,"text":"The Shulchan Aruch holds, and the Rama holds even L'Chachila as well, that wine which was either owned by a non-Jew or touched by a non-Jew, it is forbidden to derive any sort of benefit from."},{"start":85.14,"end":98.06,"text":"However, in a situation which was Bidiyavid, a significant loss, the Rama did hold that either case, whether the wine was owned by a non-Jew or wine which was touched by a non-Jew, would be permitted to benefit from."},{"start":98.06,"end":112.94,"text":"He does not allow drinking, as we mentioned, that's an issue of intermarriage, but because the libation is not so much a problem anymore, Jews don't libate, sorry, non-Jews do not libate, we do not forbid benefiting from the wine."},{"start":113.1,"end":124.36,"text":"Now even according to the Rama, who permits benefiting from Stam Yeinam nowadays, he still strongly discourages a person from making a living by selling Stam Yeinam, this is more of a Shasad Chak Heter."},{"start":124.36,"end":129.52,"text":"Okay, when it came to re-gifting a bottle of Stam Yeinam, the Shulchan Aruch clearly holds that that would be a problem."},{"start":129.82,"end":135.6,"text":"The Rama does give us a little bit more leniency, however, it would only be in a situation of a great loss,"},{"start":135.78,"end":140.32,"text":"and also we mentioned that it cannot be done in the context of a non-Jewish holiday."},{"start":140.72,"end":149.16,"text":"If it's an inexpensive bottle of wine and there's really no great loss, so even an Ashkenazi should not re-gift the bottle and should not derive any benefit."},{"start":149.16,"end":160.08,"text":"The same makhlukahs would then play out in a situation where there is a group gift being given to an employer, and the bottle of wine is going to be the gift, and the bottle of wine is Stam Yeinam."},{"start":160.22,"end":165.08,"text":"So again, the Rav Moshe would say it would be forbidden to chip into this to benefit from Stam Yeinam."},{"start":165.16,"end":173.54,"text":"However, the Rama would allow it if it's a situation of by refraining from chipping into the gift would translate into a significant loss."},{"start":173.54,"end":195.06,"text":"We discussed the question of purchasing kosher wine from a store owned by non-Jews, so that is totally permitted because in this scenario, the wine was owned by Jews originally, and it was produced in the ownership of a Jew, and therefore it's not considered Stam Yeinam, as long as the bottle is sealed, it would be considered kosher."},{"start":195.54,"end":202.6,"text":"The question really came up is purchasing wine from a Jewish store, a Jewish-owned store, which also sells Stam Yeinam."},{"start":202.66,"end":207.62,"text":"In this scenario, so the Jewish owner is not allowed to sell Stam Yeinam, he's not allowed to get benefit,"},{"start":207.78,"end":212.96,"text":"and therefore if he sold a bottle of Stam Yeinam to someone, the money which he received for it is Aser Bahana,"},{"start":213.1,"end":215.52,"text":"he's not allowed to get Hana from it, and neither is anyone else."},{"start":215.68,"end":219.7,"text":"If in this case, if you would walk into the store and purchase a kosher bottle of wine,"},{"start":219.76,"end":224.24,"text":"and use cash to purchase the bottle, then there could be a problem if you receive change,"},{"start":224.24,"end":231.62,"text":"Because the change that you're receiving, the cash change that you're receiving, could be money which was used in the transaction of Stam Yeinam, which would be Aser Behanah."},{"start":231.76,"end":238.22,"text":"The only solution we, the two solutions we gave was either use exact change or to buy with a credit card."},{"start":238.84,"end":250.04,"text":"Finally, we did mention that although this prohibition of Stam Yeinam is only rabbinic, only being very heavily in quotations, but it still is a very, very severe, it is a very severe prohibition."},{"start":250.04,"end":259.38,"text":"And although Chazal revealed one of the reasons for this gzera, there are many more unknown reasons which apply, even if the original reasons don't apply anymore, and therefore we are very machmer when it comes to Stam Yeinam."},{"start":259.6,"end":264.1,"text":"Some people go so far to consider wine which was only looked at by a non-Jew as Stam Yeinam."},{"start":264.48,"end":269.06,"text":"There are those who say that that's only in a situation where the wine is being used for a mitzvah."},{"start":269.12,"end":272.6,"text":"There are those who say it doesn't apply when the wine is in a bottle, or when the wine is sealed."},{"start":272.6,"end":282.42,"text":"Rav Yashiv Paschim, L'maisah, L'mikra Adin, according to the letter of the law, we do not forbid wine which is only looked at by a non-Jew, even to use for a mitzvah, however there are those who are machmer as a hider."}]},{"id":8,"number":8,"title":"סתם יינם — Medical Use & Yayin Mevushal","src":"/audio/2026-summer/medical-use-and-yayin-mevushal.m4a","transcript":[{"start":0.5,"end":13.76,"text":"Short and sweet number eight. We discussed how Yayin Nesech, because it is tied to Avodah Zarah, it is considered one of the three cardinal sins which a person is not allowed to do even to save a life, even in a situation of Sakhanos Nefashos."},{"start":13.88,"end":25.24,"text":"Therefore, in a situation where a doctor or someone would prescribe drinking Yayin Nesech to save a person's life, it would depend how the person or the doctor prescribed this medication, this Yayin Nesech."},{"start":25.24,"end":33.32,"text":"If the doctor says, or the person says, drink Yayin Nesech in order to save your life, then that would be forbidden, and a person would unfortunately have to give up their life."},{"start":33.48,"end":40.24,"text":"However, if the doctor or the person prescribing just says, drink wine, and the wine that they're saying to drink happens to be Yayin Nesech, but they don't say those words,"},{"start":40.4,"end":46.04,"text":"that is a situation where a person would be allowed to drink the Yayin Nesech in order to save their life."},{"start":46.04,"end":56.64,"text":"We then discuss when it came to Stam Yeinam, because Stam Yeinam is only Durat Banan, so then it may be drunk or even applied externally for life-threatening situations."},{"start":56.74,"end":61.88,"text":"In the case of Sakhanos Tafashos, we do waive Yisuri Durat Banan."},{"start":62.1,"end":67.16,"text":"However, Stam Yeinam may not be drunk for a non-life-threatening situation."},{"start":67.4,"end":72.2,"text":"If a person is sick, but it's not threatening their life, then they may not drink Stam Yeinam."},{"start":72.2,"end":78.1,"text":"The question became, what about applying Stam Yeinam externally for a non-life-threatening situation?"},{"start":78.4,"end":81.32,"text":"That was a machokas. The Shulchan Aruch held that it's still forbidden."},{"start":81.5,"end":87.96,"text":"However, the Rama said, in the case where it's non-life-threatening and it's only externally applied,"},{"start":88.24,"end":90.24,"text":"that would be a situation where it is permitted."},{"start":90.58,"end":95.34,"text":"We then discussed the topic of yayin mevushel, of cooking wine."},{"start":95.58,"end":101.4,"text":"So, if a person has a bottle of wine which is already considered Stam Yeinam, and it's already asr,"},{"start":101.4,"end":128.24,"text":"So then, we said, cooking it does not permit it, it's not going to take something which is already usher and make it permissible, however, if you have a bottle of kosher, or you have wine, which is kosher, and it's owned by a Jew, and it was cooked to a point where it's considered Yayin Mevushel, it is protected from becoming forbidden, even if a non-Jew would touch it, because we said, Yayin Mevushel, cooked wine, is not something that non-Jews libate, and therefore it is protected from being touched."},{"start":128.24,"end":137.4,"text":"The one interesting case we came up with is, let's say you have a bottle of kosher wine, which was already Mavushel, and it's owned by a Jew, it's Mavushel, and it's sealed and ready to go."},{"start":137.52,"end":141.3,"text":"But then that bottle was purchased by a non-Jew, and now the non-Jew owns the bottle of wine."},{"start":141.36,"end":143.56,"text":"He doesn't just touch it, he actually takes ownership of it."},{"start":143.66,"end":147.18,"text":"That became a machlokas, if it is forbidden or permitted to drink."},{"start":147.38,"end":153.46,"text":"Rebbe Kiva Eger held that it is forbidden, even if they don't touch the wine, but the fact that they own the wine already prohibits it."},{"start":153.56,"end":157.5,"text":"While the Rush of the Rambam and the Shulchan Aruch Passings of the Maisa, it would be permitted, even if they don't touch it."},{"start":157.5,"end":165.14,"text":"We mentioned that a case that could come up is if a person's in a restaurant, a kosher restaurant, and their boss purchases a bottle of Yayin Mevushel,"},{"start":165.24,"end":169.92,"text":"in that case, are you allowed to drink the wine? That would be subject to this machokas."},{"start":170.02,"end":177.26,"text":"We then discussed how most poskim hold that as long as the wine is heated to a temperature of Yad Tzeletisbo, it would be considered cooked."},{"start":178.04,"end":181.42,"text":"However, some hold that the wine must be heated to a point where it evaporates slightly."},{"start":181.42,"end":196.56,"text":"And lastly, when it came to pasteurized wine, there was a big machokas in the postgame, whether it's considered Mavushel or not, how hot it has to get, but we left it as a machokas, which we could use as a sniff the huckle, as a consideration, but it is ultimately a machokas."}]},{"id":9,"number":9,"title":"Wine Processing — Mixtures & Sherry Casks","src":"/audio/2026-summer/wine-mixtures-and-sherry-casks.m4a","transcript":[{"start":0.53,"end":2.23,"text":"Short and sweet number nine."},{"start":2.41,"end":9.31,"text":"We learned how if a person would mix kosher wine with other ingredients to the extent that the flavor of the wine had been affected"},{"start":9.31,"end":13.55,"text":"and it no longer tastes like wine, it would no longer be subject to the laws of Stam Yeinam,"},{"start":13.59,"end":17.03,"text":"in which case if a non-Jew would touch it, it would not become forbidden."},{"start":17.29,"end":22.45,"text":"However, we did say that the wine must be mixed well and it can no longer be visible and the flavor has to be altered."},{"start":22.57,"end":29.35,"text":"If it's not the case, and let's say for example we had was a dressing where wine and honey was mixed together"},{"start":29.35,"end":43.85,"text":"And the wine did not mix well, and it was just sitting on top of the honey, and it's visible, and it was not altered because it's not really mixed well with the honey, and then a non-Jew would touch that wine, that would be a problem of Stam Yeinam, that would be a problem and be prohibited."},{"start":43.85,"end":55.65,"text":"In a situation where non-mivushal kosher wine was mixed with water at a ratio of 1 to 6, we said that it is no longer considered wine anymore and is not subject to the laws of Stam Yeinam."},{"start":55.75,"end":63.37,"text":"If a person would freeze or congeal wine to an extent where it's a salad, it is no longer subject to the laws of Stam Yeinam. It's not something that would be libated."},{"start":63.57,"end":72.73,"text":"However, once it melts and it's back in its liquid form, it is subject to the laws of Stam Yeinam, and we were not really sure exactly at what point it's considered a liquid again."},{"start":72.73,"end":76.57,"text":"The example we said was a slushy. Is that a solid? Is that a liquid?"},{"start":76.71,"end":80.83,"text":"So a person should be careful if they have a frozen wine at an event, which is Namavushel,"},{"start":81.29,"end":87.25,"text":"they should make sure not to allow any non-juice to come in contact with it as it starts to melt."},{"start":87.49,"end":94.63,"text":"We said that if a person has pottery or a barrel which had absorbed Stam Yeinam into the walls,"},{"start":94.75,"end":101.75,"text":"and to the extent that if the water was left in the barrel, it would then get flavor from the wine,"},{"start":101.75,"end":111.91,"text":"So then, the water would be forbidden from getting any benefit from, it has the flavor of the Stam Yeinam, which was absorbed in the walls of the pottery or the barrel, and therefore would be prohibited to benefit."},{"start":112.11,"end":114.97,"text":"Which brought us to the next conversation of sherry cask whiskey."},{"start":115.17,"end":123.09,"text":"We said sherry cask whiskey is produced by aging the whiskey in barrels which had been used in the past to store sherry wine, which of course is Stam Yeinam."},{"start":123.27,"end":127.69,"text":"The question was, is this going to be prohibited or permissible?"},{"start":127.69,"end":132.23,"text":"We had many reasons to forbid this sherry cask whiskey."},{"start":132.55,"end":138.87,"text":"The first reason was because, again, as we just mentioned, the sherry gets absorbed into the barrel,"},{"start":138.97,"end":145.47,"text":"and then when the whiskey sits in the barrel for at least more than 24 hours, it would then get some flavor from the sherry."},{"start":145.63,"end":149.79,"text":"So the first question we said, well, is the flavor buttle in the whiskey?"},{"start":149.93,"end":154.95,"text":"Well, according to the Shach, the whiskey must be 60 times the sherry flavor,"},{"start":154.95,"end":173.17,"text":"We also then had to discuss, well, how much sherry? Do we have to be Mibato? Is the entire width of the barrel filled with the sherry flavor? Or is it just a Kedek Lipa? Well, if you go with the Shach's understanding of the Shulchan Aruch, it's the entire width of the barrel."},{"start":173.25,"end":180.47,"text":"Another reason to forbid it, because there is no way you are going to have 60 times the entire width of a barrel with the contents of the barrel."},{"start":180.47,"end":188.25,"text":"Another reason to ask or to forbid is we said that since the sherry is presumably being added to the whiskey,"},{"start":188.43,"end":194.03,"text":"you specifically want the whiskey to sit in the sherry cask in order to get flavor,"},{"start":194.33,"end":197.17,"text":"and something which is added for flavor is never bitul,"},{"start":197.21,"end":202.29,"text":"so even if you would have 60 times the amount, it would never be bitul because it is specifically being done to add flavor."},{"start":202.89,"end":208.27,"text":"Similarly, another issue was anything that is added for chazusa, for color, is never bitul,"},{"start":208.27,"end":216.11,"text":"And if, again, we assume that the sherry cask serves a function of adding color to the whiskey, that would also introduce another issue."},{"start":216.21,"end":223.79,"text":"And lastly, even if a person would come up with a scenario where the sherry is batal, it is nullified in the whiskey,"},{"start":223.95,"end":229.29,"text":"but then we said there could be a problem of purchasing this whiskey by relying on the bitul,"},{"start":229.31,"end":235.31,"text":"which could be a problem of imi batal and isser l'chachila, of deliberately nullifying a prohibition, which is a problem."}]},{"id":10,"number":10,"title":"Wine Processing — Sherry Casks, המשכה & Grape Products","src":"/audio/2026-summer/sherry-casks-hamshacha-and-grape-products.m4a","transcript":[{"start":0.5,"end":10.96,"text":"Short and sweet number 10. We proceeded to discuss all the many possible reasons why we can permit buying and drinking sherry cast whiskey."},{"start":11.26,"end":20.82,"text":"So first of all, in the problem that we presented of bitul, of the Shach's opinion that a person would need 60 times the sherry flavor to allow the whiskey,"},{"start":20.82,"end":33.94,"text":"That was the Shach's opinion, but the Shulchan Aruch and the Taz both disagree, and they hold that only a ratio of 1 to 6 of sherry to whiskey would be required, and in most cases of sherry-cast whiskey, we would have a 1 to 6 ratio."},{"start":34.26,"end":47.68,"text":"We also said that the Rav Moshe pointed out that even according to the Shach, in a case where whiskey is being used to mimibatil the sherry, and its whiskey has a very sharp taste, it could be that even according to the Shach, only a 1 to 6 ratio is necessary."},{"start":47.68,"end":64.86,"text":"And even in the Shach's stringent opinion, we said that buying a second fill sherry cask whiskey would be permitted, permissible, because in the case of second fills, the barrels have not been used to store sherry wine for over 12 months, and even the Shach would agree that that would be permissible."},{"start":64.86,"end":77.26,"text":"Okay, in response to the issue of an ingredient which is added specifically for flavor, which is never vatil, Rav Moshe Feinstein told us that that's only in a situation where the ingredient adds a significant strong flavor."},{"start":77.36,"end":93.22,"text":"But in a case where wine is being mixed into whiskey, and Rav Moshe was even talking about blended whiskey, which is actual wine, but he said since it's only giving a weak flavor, it's not subject to this rule, and it can be vatil if you had enough of a ratio to mimivatil it."},{"start":93.22,"end":100.06,"text":"We had another svar from the Mishnah Halachos, and he said that really a sherry cask whiskey, the sherry is not even giving flavor to the whiskey."},{"start":100.14,"end":108.78,"text":"The whole purpose of the sherry cask is that the sherry will cover up the bad flavor of the oak barrel, and therefore improve the quality of the whiskey."},{"start":108.92,"end":114.26,"text":"But it's not even giving flavor, the sherry is not giving the flavor to the whiskey, and that's why it would be permissible according to him."},{"start":114.68,"end":122.56,"text":"In response to the issue of an ingredient which is added for color, so even if we do take that assumption that the sherry is giving color,"},{"start":122.56,"end":128.7,"text":"So the only time we say that it's not bitul is when it's being added for color and the ingredient is an isser de areissa."},{"start":128.78,"end":135.5,"text":"Over here we're dealing with Stam Yeinam, which is only an isser de rabbanon, in which case it would not be subject to that rule and could be bitul."},{"start":135.62,"end":144.82,"text":"We wanted to respond to the issue of the mevatl isser lech achila, of relying on mevatl isser lech achila to buy this bottle of sherry."},{"start":144.92,"end":147.14,"text":"So we said two reasons why that's not a problem."},{"start":147.14,"end":160.6,"text":"First of all, the companies are not producing this for Jewish people, and they're not purposely nullifying the sherry into the whiskey in order to allow it for Jews, in which case that would not be considered relying on bitul l'chachila."},{"start":160.78,"end":171.44,"text":"Also, Rav Moshe pointed out a fascinating sfar. He said that the only time we say that it's a problem to be a bitul l'chachila is where the isser, even an isser d'arbanan, is linked to an isser d'araysa."},{"start":171.44,"end":181.06,"text":"But in this case, we have the opinion of the Rama and others who say that the Yisr of Stam Yeinam is no longer linked to any Yisr De Reisah."},{"start":181.1,"end":184.2,"text":"It's not linked to Yayin Nesech because non-Jews don't lie about it anymore."},{"start":184.36,"end":190.32,"text":"In which case, the whole Yisr nowadays is an Yisr De Rabbanah but not linked to an Yisr De Reisah."},{"start":190.4,"end":195.1,"text":"And that type of prohibition is permissible to be Mibatot L'Chad Chila."},{"start":195.22,"end":201.18,"text":"Okay, the Halach HaLamayisah we came out for, Sherry Cass Whiskey, came out to be a pretty large Machlokas."},{"start":201.44,"end":218.76,"text":"Rav Moshe Feinstein was quoted as saying that it is allowed, but a bal nefesh, a person who wants to be makhmur, should be makhmur, although Rav Dovah Feinstein reported that Rav Moshe was only talking about blended whiskey, but sherry casks, Rav Moshe, he assumes, would allow and would permit and not have to be makhmur."},{"start":218.86,"end":230.14,"text":"Rav Belsky was very makhmur on sherry cask whiskey, and he said it is absolutely forbidden, that is the OU policy, following Rav Belsky, Rav Asher Weiss, Rav Mishnah Halachos, Rav Shalom Miller, all hold that sherry cask whiskey is permitted."},{"start":230.14,"end":248.82,"text":"We then discuss grape juice, making grape juice. So when a person is in the process of making grape juice, the juice or the wine, which it ultimately will become, is subject to the isser of Stam Yeinam after the juice has been intentionally separated from the skins and the pits of the grapes."},{"start":248.82,"end":266.84,"text":"So in a situation where a person is crushing grapes in a bucket, which does not have a hole in it, and all the skins and the pits are all mixed together, and they have not been separated intentionally, so then they are not susceptible to becoming Stam Yeinam, and would be protected from being in contact with a non-Jew."},{"start":266.84,"end":272.1,"text":"We discussed Grappa, Brandy, Cognac, which is all products of grape juice and wine."},{"start":272.22,"end":274.16,"text":"We said they all need a reliable hexer."},{"start":274.22,"end":277.38,"text":"They all would be subject to Stam Yeinam."},{"start":278.48,"end":282.96,"text":"However, after they've been produced, if a non-jew touched them, they would still be permissible"},{"start":283.42,"end":285.08,"text":"because they are considered Mavushal."},{"start":285.12,"end":289.66,"text":"Through the process of their being made, they are cooked and also have a considerably different taste than wine."}]},{"id":11,"number":11,"title":"Social Drinking","src":"/audio/2026-summer/social-drinking.m4a","transcript":[{"start":0.21,"end":1.75,"text":"Short and sweet number 11."},{"start":2.05,"end":12.07,"text":"The Shulchan Aruch, as understood by the Gra, says that it is forbidden to drink any alcoholic or prestigious non-alcoholic beverages in a non-Jewish establishment."},{"start":12.51,"end":19.21,"text":"Now this Gezerah we discussed was a later Gezerah than the Isser of B'shalakum and Pasakum,"},{"start":19.29,"end":24.25,"text":"and therefore it is not subject to the same level of restrictions as those previous Gezerahs."},{"start":24.51,"end":29.51,"text":"One exception would be that if the drink was removed from the non-Jews domain,"},{"start":29.51,"end":33.55,"text":"Again, assuming it's a kosher beverage, then it would be allowed."},{"start":33.77,"end":35.43,"text":"Now, how far does it have to be removed?"},{"start":35.49,"end":44.45,"text":"The Gra, Paschan, that the beverage must be brought into the Jew's domain, the Rishus, whether that's their home or a hotel room, it has to be that far removed."},{"start":44.67,"end":48.39,"text":"However, the Darken Tshuva says that it only needs to be taken out of the non-Jew's domain."},{"start":48.47,"end":51.17,"text":"It doesn't have to be brought all the way to the Rishus of the Jew."},{"start":51.53,"end":58.19,"text":"We did say that everyone agrees that if it's a designated outdoor eating area of a non-Jewish bar, that's still considered inside of the non-Jew's domain."},{"start":58.19,"end":62.01,"text":"The Shach told us that this prohibition does have an exception."},{"start":62.15,"end":69.01,"text":"In a case where it would cause animosity not to drink in this establishment, then it would be permissible to drink."},{"start":69.13,"end":76.63,"text":"As well as we mentioned, another exception is if it's a sporadic and inconsistent manner of drinking, it would be permitted."},{"start":76.83,"end":81.49,"text":"So the example we said was if a person does not sit down, they're just walking around with a drink, and this is not frequent,"},{"start":81.59,"end":87.61,"text":"this is a one-time or very infrequent occurrence, then it would be permissible."},{"start":87.61,"end":93.99,"text":"The Huchas Katanos, the Kaf HaChaim, actually said that the Shulchan Aruch holds that non-alcoholic beverages are permitted."},{"start":94.15,"end":103.03,"text":"He only forbids alcoholic drinks, but the Groth said that the Shulchan Aruch held even non-alcoholic drinks are problematic if they are prestigious."},{"start":103.29,"end":108.77,"text":"We have the opinion of the Ramah, who held that only date beer is prohibited in a non-Jewish establishment."},{"start":109.03,"end":112.99,"text":"Date beer was what was drunk in the times of the Gemara, and that was the only thing that was prohibited."},{"start":112.99,"end":118.33,"text":"All other beverages, according to the Rama, would be permitted in a non-Jewish establishment or a non-Jewish bar."},{"start":118.55,"end":126.25,"text":"The question of conducting a business meeting over a beer at a non-Jewish bar, we said, is a machukes or Moshe Feinstein held."},{"start":126.31,"end":136.29,"text":"It would be allowed because we'd have to avoid causing any animosity with the non-Jewish employee or boss or co-worker."},{"start":136.43,"end":138.81,"text":"In a situation where there's no animosity, it would not be allowed."},{"start":138.81,"end":143.67,"text":"But to avoid it, avoid animosity, we will allow it."},{"start":143.71,"end":145.71,"text":"The Orletzion, however, said it would be forbidden."},{"start":146.35,"end":151.41,"text":"However, he did say that if it was non-alcoholic drinks that were being drunk at the bar, it would be permissible."},{"start":151.63,"end":158.39,"text":"We then even discussed the question of drinking coffee, which is a prestigious drink, albeit not alcoholic, but it is a prestigious drink."},{"start":158.51,"end":161.39,"text":"Can a person drink a coffee at a non-Jewish-owned coffee shop?"},{"start":161.59,"end":164.59,"text":"So the Shulchan Aruch, as understood by the Gra, would say that it's forbidden."},{"start":164.59,"end":169.57,"text":"We said that the Halachas Kitanos and the Kaf HaChaim would say it's permitted because the drink is non-alcoholic."},{"start":169.63,"end":176.27,"text":"The Rama, we said, would be subject to a Machlokas, the Pischei Teshuva, Chochmas Adam, even the Vada Arbarotzo is a very early source,"},{"start":176.43,"end":180.99,"text":"say that in the Rama, even according to the Rama, it would be forbidden to drink the coffee."},{"start":181.09,"end":186.69,"text":"However, Rehershal Schachter did tell us that even though one should follow this Chochmas Adam,"},{"start":186.85,"end":191.77,"text":"if it's not common to socialize with strangers at a coffee shop, there's room to be lenient."},{"start":191.77,"end":195.61,"text":"Okay, then we discussed the question of attending a non-Jewish party."},{"start":195.81,"end":198.65,"text":"So, again, this party is being arranged by non-Jews."},{"start":198.69,"end":203.73,"text":"So, in a situation where it's being arranged by non-Jews and the majority of attendees are non-Jewish,"},{"start":203.77,"end":208.45,"text":"Rav Moshe Feinstein said it would be forbidden to be at that party."},{"start":210.33,"end":216.23,"text":"However, the one exception Rav Moshe said is if there would be animosity,"},{"start":216.39,"end":219.27,"text":"it would be permitted to attend and even to drink an alcoholic drink."},{"start":219.27,"end":226.93,"text":"The Orletzion held that it would be permitted to attend this party, but not to drink alcohol, even in a situation of animosity."},{"start":227.07,"end":231.67,"text":"So that would be the Machlokas subject between the Rav Moshe and the Orletzion."},{"start":231.85,"end":236.89,"text":"We did mention, though, if the party is being organized by non-Jews, but the majority of attendees are Jewish,"},{"start":237.07,"end":241.35,"text":"so then we said everyone would agree that it would be permitted to attend and even to drink alcohol."},{"start":241.71,"end":245.39,"text":"The question of attending a non-Jewish wedding feast was Machlokas, the Taz said,"},{"start":245.39,"end":249.25,"text":"It's an Issa Dar Esa, and you're not allowed to attend, even if it's going to cause animosity, you cannot go."},{"start":249.41,"end":254.49,"text":"The Shach, however, said it's only an Issa Midr HaBanan, and therefore, if it will cause animosity to not attend, it would be permitted."},{"start":254.75,"end":259.35,"text":"The Sefer B'ni Yisroel Amin said that if the Jews have their own table, it would be permissible as well."},{"start":259.53,"end":267.23,"text":"And we said if a Ger wanted to attend their parents' birthday party, or even a regular meal of their parents, it would be permissible,"},{"start":268.11,"end":270.41,"text":"even though there are non-Jews present, because they are Ger."},{"start":270.41,"end":278.53,"text":"But we said it would be permissible for a couple of factors, including the fact that mitzvah keep it of an aim, and that would be permissible as well."}]},{"id":12,"number":12,"title":"Wine Contact — Who Touched the Wine?","src":"/audio/2026-summer/wine-contact-who-touched.m4a","transcript":[{"start":0.21,"end":11.65,"text":"Short and sweet number 12. In a situation where it was possible that a non-mevushal open bottle of wine was touched by a non-Jew, it depends on the type of non-Jew who possibly touched it."},{"start":11.73,"end":21.09,"text":"If it was possibly touched by a Christian, so then Rav Ovadya told us that for the Sfardim, the wine is forbidden to drink and even to get benefit from, while the Rama held that wine would be permissible."},{"start":21.25,"end":24.91,"text":"If it was possibly touched by a Muslim, everybody agreed that the wine is permitted."},{"start":24.91,"end":36.71,"text":"We then discussed the question of irreligious Jews, particularly Jews who are Mechalei Shabbos B'Far Hesya, which means that they desecrate Shabbos publicly in front of ten people at least three times."},{"start":36.91,"end":48.89,"text":"So then we know that for the Din of the Gemara, that any wine that is touched by a Mechalei Shabbos B'Far Hesya, the wine is forbidden to drink, but it still is permitted to get benefit from."},{"start":49.01,"end":50.85,"text":"However, again, we cannot drink it."},{"start":50.85,"end":58.13,"text":"Or Shternbach does tell us that it's best not to get benefit from the wine if there's no need, but the minimal is that the wine would be prohibited to drink."},{"start":58.27,"end":62.87,"text":"We discussed what is considered a Michal Shabbos B'Far Hesya b'Zman Hazet in current times."},{"start":62.99,"end":68.13,"text":"So we said that there are really basically, in the general sense, there are four types of irreligious Jews."},{"start":68.31,"end":69.99,"text":"The first type is the traditional Jew."},{"start":70.07,"end":76.99,"text":"That would be someone who, although they are not Shabbos observant, they will go to shul, they recite Kiddush, they will do traditional practices."},{"start":76.99,"end":80.65,"text":"However, they just don't keep Shabbos and they transgress it publicly."},{"start":80.91,"end":85.03,"text":"The next category was what we call the respectful Jew."},{"start":85.19,"end":87.29,"text":"That's someone who, again, they do not keep Shabbos."},{"start":87.31,"end":92.35,"text":"They will be Michal Shabbos before Heser in public, but they would not do so in front of a Rav or someone that they respect."},{"start":92.55,"end":96.69,"text":"The third category was the totally unaffiliated Jew or the Tinov SheNizhba."},{"start":96.91,"end":99.31,"text":"They do not have a Jewish upbringing."},{"start":99.63,"end":100.57,"text":"They do not have a Jewish education."},{"start":100.85,"end":103.69,"text":"They will deliberately transgress Shabbos even in front of a Rav."},{"start":103.69,"end":110.69,"text":"But again, they're not doing it out of spite, they just did not have the proper education or sensitivity to Shabbos."},{"start":110.75,"end":115.99,"text":"The last category was the Mummer L'Hachas, a person who is the anti-religious Jew."},{"start":116.11,"end":122.71,"text":"He had a Jewish education, they rejected it, and are now deliberately transgressing Shabbos, and they would do so even in front of a Rav."},{"start":123.09,"end":128.01,"text":"So the status of the first three, the traditional Jews, the respectful Jews, and the Tinek Shinizhba,"},{"start":128.15,"end":132.95,"text":"we said was a machlokas between the poskim, whether or not they would prohibit wine that they touched."},{"start":132.95,"end":142.13,"text":"However, we did say that the Mumra L'Chachis, this anti-religious Jew, if he would touch wine, everybody agrees that is a case that the wine would be forbidden to drink."}]},{"id":13,"number":13,"title":"Wine Contact — Four Conditions","src":"/audio/2026-summer/wine-contact-four-conditions.m4a","transcript":[{"start":0.34,"end":5.98,"text":"Short and sweet number 13. There are four criteria that must be met in order to make wine"},{"start":5.98,"end":11.38,"text":"which is touched by a non-Jew to be forbidden both for drinking and for benefit. The first one is that"},{"start":11.38,"end":16.32,"text":"when they touch the wine they have to have intent to touch the wine. The second is that they must"},{"start":16.32,"end":20.74,"text":"know that the liquid that they are touching is wine. They have to be touching the wine for the"},{"start":20.74,"end":24.34,"text":"sake of the wine, not for some other reason. For example, removing the foam from the top of the"},{"start":24.34,"end":27.62,"text":"And finally, they have to have shaken the wine."},{"start":27.74,"end":34.78,"text":"If there is a scenario where one of these four criteria are missing, and the non-Jew is a bona fide idol worshiper,"},{"start":34.9,"end":38.84,"text":"the wine is permitted to benefit, but it would still be forbidden to drink."},{"start":38.96,"end":43.88,"text":"If one of the factors are missing and the Jew is not an idol worshiper, for example, a Muslim,"},{"start":44.04,"end":46.36,"text":"then the wine would be permitted even to drink."},{"start":46.74,"end":51.14,"text":"The question is, what about a situation where the person is a Christian?"},{"start":51.14,"end":62.48,"text":"So then we mentioned that according to the Shulchan Aruch, Christians are considered idol worshippers, and all that we just mentioned previously about an idol worshipper would apply to a Christian."},{"start":62.64,"end":70.14,"text":"We said, though, however, the Ramah holds that Christians nowadays do not laibate, and therefore they are not considered to be idol worshippers."},{"start":70.14,"end":82.76,"text":"The Shach himself says that the Ramah is only lenient, sorry, the Shach is lenient even without a loss of money, but the Shach says in the case of a Christian, we should not be, Megad, we should not be lenient if there is not a loss of money."},{"start":82.96,"end":98.08,"text":"The present custom in regards to this, about a Christian touching a non-mubishal wine, the Svaradim do hold that they are considered idol worshippers, and the Ashkenazim generally follow the Shach, and therefore would only allow wine which is poured by a Christian in the case of a great loss of money."},{"start":98.08,"end":102.98,"text":"We then discussed some other cases where it would be permissible to drink the wine, according to everyone."},{"start":103.08,"end":112.74,"text":"If, let's say, the non-Jew came into indirect contact with the wine, and also it was unintentional contact, that would be a case that would be permissible."},{"start":112.92,"end":118.6,"text":"Another case that everyone allows, if the non-Jew just touches the bottle, but doesn't move it, doesn't pick it up, that would be permissible."},{"start":118.82,"end":123.04,"text":"If they would shake a closed bottle, that would also be permissible."},{"start":123.04,"end":134.28,"text":"We discussed a case where, let's say, they shook the bottle without lifting it, so that the Shulchan Aruch did say the wine is prohibited, while the Rama would say it's permitted if there is a great loss."}]},{"id":14,"number":14,"title":"Wine Contact — Pouring, Clinking & Accidental Contact","src":"/audio/2026-summer/wine-contact-pouring-clinking-and-accidental-contact.m4a","transcript":[{"start":0.5,"end":11.2,"text":"Short and sweet number 14. We said that based on everything we learned, we did not understand why is the common custom that wine poured by a Muslim or an irreligious Jew is prohibited to drink."},{"start":11.34,"end":26.24,"text":"The confusion mainly stems from the fact that we said that if one of the four criteria are missing, and the person who poured the wine was a non-idol worshipping individual, such as a Muslim or an irreligious Jew,"},{"start":26.24,"end":32.8,"text":"So because of the concept of Nachas Dargah, of going down one level, the wine should be permissible to benefit and to drink from."},{"start":32.96,"end":38.42,"text":"So Rav Ovadya gave us one approach, and he said that, at least in the context of the Shulchan Aruch,"},{"start":38.54,"end":42.6,"text":"the Shulchan Aruch, he says, really holds that be'etzem, really inherently,"},{"start":42.96,"end":47.9,"text":"wine which is poured by an idol worshiper is prohibited both to drink and to benefit from."},{"start":48.04,"end":51.22,"text":"And therefore, even if you're missing the criteria of shaking,"},{"start":77.74,"end":87.04,"text":"We saw from the Ashkenazic postgame, Rav Moshe Feinstein, Rav Shulchan Aruch, Rav Ovadya Yosef, they all said that the minug is that wine poured by religious Jews and Muslims is still forbidden to drink."},{"start":87.22,"end":94.14,"text":"We had a couple of different reasons. Possibly number one is that we're Mach Melech Tataz, the pouring still would be prohibited, even if the person is not an idol worshiper."},{"start":94.28,"end":100.28,"text":"The second one is that deliberate pouring is considered shaking, and there's no way to really get around that."},{"start":100.32,"end":106.92,"text":"If the wine was deliberately poured, that's considered shaking, and you could have all four requirements to prohibit the wine."},{"start":107.18,"end":114.84,"text":"We also had Rav Shulman Zalman Orbach who told us that when the wine is poured by a Muslim, even the wine which is remaining in the bottle is forbidden."},{"start":115.02,"end":121.18,"text":"And Rav Yosef said that even when it was poured by an irreligious Jew, the wine which is remaining in the bottle is forbidden."},{"start":121.38,"end":124.18,"text":"We had two different ways of understanding Rav Yosef."},{"start":124.3,"end":130.94,"text":"The first one was that although we do say Nachas Dargah to allow the wine, that's in benefit."},{"start":131.1,"end":135.36,"text":"So that we said that's only in a situation where you had non-Jews."},{"start":135.36,"end":145.22,"text":"The whole concept of Nachas Daigah was only applied to non-Jews, but irreligious Jews never had this concept of Nachas Daigah applied, and therefore the remaining bottle in the white would be forbidden."},{"start":145.38,"end":158.08,"text":"Another reason to explain Rav Ovadya Yosef is that he can hold that pouring is considered shaking, like we mentioned before, and therefore we view it as if they did shake the bottle, and the wine inside the bottle is forbidden for that reason as well."},{"start":158.08,"end":167.06,"text":"We did say that because of all the factors that we've learned to this point, and another possible consideration of Kavod HaBrios,"},{"start":167.16,"end":172.98,"text":"that if a person would have an irreligious Jew pour them non-mimitial wine, and by not drinking it, it will cause them to be embarrassed."},{"start":173.24,"end":175.22,"text":"And again, a Kavod HaBrios issue."},{"start":175.36,"end":179.66,"text":"So then there is possible room to be lenient and to drink the wine that was poured."},{"start":179.84,"end":183.08,"text":"However, if they won't be embarrassed, the best thing is to avoid it."},{"start":183.12,"end":186.38,"text":"The minik is not to drink it and to get around not drinking that wine."},{"start":186.38,"end":191.3,"text":"We mentioned the question of clinking glasses with a non-Jew."},{"start":191.38,"end":195.94,"text":"So we said, for many reasons, if a person would clink their glass with a non-Jew,"},{"start":196.02,"end":200.62,"text":"even if the Jewish person's glass had non-mubushel wine in it, the wine would stay permitted."},{"start":200.84,"end":205.88,"text":"First of all, we said the movement of the wine when you clink the glasses is not really considered shaking."},{"start":205.96,"end":207.76,"text":"It's just a slight movement."},{"start":208.06,"end":210.1,"text":"The second of all is, even if you want to say it's shaking,"},{"start":210.24,"end":214.52,"text":"but since it's the Jewish person's movement and the non-Jews together,"},{"start":217.78,"end":224.6,"text":"We said that when a non-Jew and a Jew pour wine together, the wine is permissible, and certainly when they just clink the glasses together."},{"start":225.08,"end":231.66,"text":"We said that if, let's say, a non-Jew accidentally touched non-Mavushal wine, so based on what we learned in the past already,"},{"start":231.84,"end":234.72,"text":"if the person was a Muslim, it would be permitted to drink."},{"start":234.8,"end":237.92,"text":"If they're a Christian, it would be subject to the Mahalokas between the Shulchan Aruch and the Ramah."},{"start":238.0,"end":242.96,"text":"The Shulchan Aruch would say it's forbidden to drink, but you can allow it to get benefit from it, and the Ramah would say it's permitted to drink."},{"start":242.96,"end":248.76,"text":"And the question of drinking non-militant wine on an LL flight, it was very much not recommended."},{"start":248.84,"end":251.98,"text":"If it was being poured by an irreligious flight attendant, there are those who allow it."},{"start":252.04,"end":254.6,"text":"However, we said it would not be recommended to do so."}]},{"id":15,"number":15,"title":"Wine Contact — נצוק & Unattended Wine","src":"/audio/2026-summer/nitzok-and-unattended-wine.m4a","transcript":[{"start":0.34,"end":21.8,"text":"So in suite number 15, based on everything we just learned previously, we said that when it comes to going to a restaurant where an irreligious Jewish waiter will be the one to open the bottle of wine and possibly even pouring the wine or shaking the wine, so we said the best thing is to avoid it and don't rely on all the leniencies that we said before because it's avoidable."},{"start":21.8,"end":26.58,"text":"however if it's unavoidable and the waiter will be offended to tell him that he's not allowed to"},{"start":26.58,"end":31.82,"text":"open your bottle so then we said there are there is grounds to allow it if a irreligious parent"},{"start":31.82,"end":36.56,"text":"would recite Kiddush drink from the cup and then offer the wine to their religious child again the"},{"start":36.56,"end":40.74,"text":"best thing would be is to avoid drinking it if it's impossible there are there is ground to"},{"start":40.74,"end":48.48,"text":"be lenient to drink it as well okay now when we discuss the concept of Nitzuk so Nitzuk is the"},{"start":48.48,"end":55.92,"text":"The definition of Nitzok is when kosher wine is poured into a cup or a container which contains non-kosher wine"},{"start":55.92,"end":64.14,"text":"and the stream that is going between the bottle which is pouring into the container or the cup is completely unbroken."},{"start":64.2,"end":65.42,"text":"It is an unbroken stream."},{"start":66.16,"end":75.84,"text":"In this scenario, we view it as if the wine which is inside the cup or the container is now inside of the bottle which was being poured from into"},{"start":75.84,"end":82.86,"text":"and potentially can forbid the bottle itself that is being poured out of now the question is what kind"},{"start":82.86,"end":88.62,"text":"of what type of wine are we talking about that's inside of the cup so first of all the the wine"},{"start":88.62,"end":94.66,"text":"whatever it is it must be wet enough that it would be uh it's wide enough to get your hand wet which"},{"start":94.66,"end":99.44,"text":"could then wet something else and also when we say that it's non-kosher wine so the Shach told us that"},{"start":99.44,"end":104.14,"text":"has to be that it's wine which is forbidden both from drinking and from benefit if it's only"},{"start":121.46,"end":125.56,"text":"The Rama does say that Nitzuk would apply to wine which is touched by a Christian."},{"start":125.7,"end":131.12,"text":"If the wine in the cup would only be prohibited from being in contact with a Muslim or an irreligious Jew though,"},{"start":131.22,"end":135.22,"text":"so then Nitzuk does not apply because it is certainly permitted to benefit from that wine"},{"start":135.22,"end":137.74,"text":"and therefore Nitzuk does not apply to that scenario."},{"start":138.4,"end":143.14,"text":"In a case of great loss of money where the bottle that was being poured from was very expensive,"},{"start":143.34,"end":149.92,"text":"so then the Shulchan Aruch said that we would allow the bottle that was poured from, we would not apply Nitzuk."},{"start":149.92,"end":154.24,"text":"The Rama did qualify those by saying there has to be a case of significant loss."},{"start":154.38,"end":159.92,"text":"If it's just a small amount of wine or it's inexpensive wine, so then we would apply Nitzuk and we would forbid the bottle that was poured from."},{"start":160.06,"end":167.2,"text":"We also mentioned another leniency from the Rama, that the Rama said we can use the concept of bitul peshishim in the context of Nitzuk."},{"start":167.32,"end":176.0,"text":"So again, so we view as if the contents of the cup that are being poured into are now inside the bottle that is being poured from."},{"start":202.72,"end":204.7,"text":"bitul b'shishim"},{"start":206.0,"end":209.3,"text":"We're relying on the fact that the bottle will be 60 times the wine that's inside the cup."},{"start":209.38,"end":212.08,"text":"That would be Mevat Ezzel Chachila, which we say is prohibited."},{"start":212.82,"end":217.8,"text":"If let's say a person was pouring kosher Mevushal wine into a cup which is containing forbidden wine,"},{"start":217.96,"end":220.18,"text":"it still could become prohibited."},{"start":221.32,"end":227.34,"text":"The Mevushal wine can become prohibited because although it's Mevushal and protected from being touched by a non-Jew,"},{"start":227.94,"end":233.26,"text":"again, because Nitzuk is viewed as if the wine in the cup is being mixed with the wine in the bottle,"},{"start":233.26,"end":236.78,"text":"then being Mavushel would not protect it"},{"start":236.78,"end":240.28,"text":"if a non-Jew would ever pour wine to dispose of it"},{"start":240.28,"end":241.14,"text":"they're pouring it down the drain"},{"start":241.14,"end":242.6,"text":"so then the wine would be permitted"},{"start":242.6,"end":246.02,"text":"that's not subject to the rules of Stam Yeinam"},{"start":246.02,"end":251.48,"text":"also if a non-Jew would touch wine with the sole intent of making it forbidden to Jews"},{"start":251.48,"end":254.82,"text":"they were really trying to make the Jews upset and ask for their wine"},{"start":254.82,"end":255.96,"text":"so then it would be permitted"},{"start":255.96,"end":257.92,"text":"and it should even be drunk in front of them"},{"start":257.92,"end":259.84,"text":"to show them that they were not successful"},{"start":259.84,"end":267.28,"text":"We then introduced the topic of leaving wine unattended with non-Jews."},{"start":267.3,"end":272.82,"text":"So there are two real concerns when leaving wine unsupervised with a non-Jew."},{"start":272.88,"end":278.8,"text":"First of all, we are concerned that the non-Jew may touch the wine with the intent to libate and therefore can prohibit the wine that way."},{"start":278.88,"end":287.22,"text":"Also, we are concerned that perhaps the non-Jew exchanged the kosher wine with forbidden wine, and now the wine inside of the bottle is actually prohibited."},{"start":288.0,"end":310.72,"text":"Regarding this second concern that the non-Jew had exchanged the wine, so there has to be, this does take some time for the non-Jew to go get forbidden wine and then to make that exchange, and therefore we assume it would take a little bit longer than a mil for him to do that, in which case, again, we would not allow wine to be left with a non-Jew, especially not for a time that's longer than a mil."},{"start":310.72,"end":318.18,"text":"However, if let's say a person did leave an open bottle of wine with a non-Jew, unintended."},{"start":318.54,"end":324.74,"text":"So in a case where the non-Jew was a Christian or a bona fide idol worshiper,"},{"start":324.84,"end":330.4,"text":"so then the Shulchan Aruch says that the wine is forbidden, even if you just left for a few seconds."},{"start":330.52,"end":334.44,"text":"Again, the concern is that the Christian or idol worshiper had touched the bottle of wine"},{"start":334.44,"end":338.28,"text":"and made it forbidden because he had intent to libate."},{"start":338.28,"end":348.82,"text":"The Rama did say that because Christians nowadays have the status of Muslims and are non-idol worshippers, he does not go as far as Shulchan Aruch, and he would not prohibit the wine immediately, it would take much longer."},{"start":348.96,"end":367.88,"text":"But in a situation where the wine was left with a Christian, according to the Rama, or even according to Shulchan Aruch, if it was left with a Muslim, or an irreligious Jew, or any other non-idol worshipping individual, so if it's in the home of a Jew, and there's no other non-kosher wine around, so the concern is that maybe they switched it with non-kosher wine."},{"start":367.88,"end":382.62,"text":"In which case, it would only be prohibited if it was left with them for the time that it takes to walk a mill, which is more than 18 minutes or more than 24 minutes, but anything less than that, we would not be concerned that they had swapped out the wine."},{"start":382.62,"end":389.8,"text":"If there is a concern that the non-Jew may drink from the wine or make direct contact with it for their own benefit,"},{"start":389.96,"end":399.18,"text":"so then again the wine becomes forbidden immediately, assuming that they are someone who would cause the wine to be prohibited if they would come in direct contact with it."}]},{"id":16,"number":16,"title":"Unattended Wine — Seals, יוצא ונכנס & Monitoring","src":"/audio/2026-summer/unattended-wine-seals-and-monitoring.m4a","transcript":[{"start":0.24,"end":6.82,"text":"Short and sweet number 16. We presented many different solutions for leaving wine unattended with a non-Jew."},{"start":6.9,"end":15.58,"text":"The first solution was if the bottle of wine has a double seal, so once it has been double sealed, it may be left with a non-Jew unsupervised."},{"start":15.9,"end":27.42,"text":"Ramosha told us that even a single seal, but that is almost impossible to replicate, would be considered a double seal and would protect the wine from being left unsupervised with a non-Jew."},{"start":27.42,"end":42.76,"text":"We said that metal screw caps on a bottle would be considered a double seal because the moment that they are opened, the ring breaks off and the only way to reseal the bottle would be to bring it back to the factory, which is almost impossible, in which case it's also considered a double seal."},{"start":42.88,"end":56.98,"text":"A cork with a foil wrapper around it is also considered a double seal. The cork alone in the bottle is just one seal. Some people actually held that it was a double seal because the cork breaks when it's opened and therefore it's also impossible to replicate."},{"start":57.42,"end":65.92,"text":"Even those who did not agree held that a champagne cork would be considered a double seal because it is pretty much impossible to get back in once it has been taken out."},{"start":66.14,"end":74.3,"text":"If a person needed to create a double seal, they can put the bottle of wine in a plastic bag and tie the plastic bag very tightly around the bottle,"},{"start":74.36,"end":80.04,"text":"put tape around the bag, which can tear very easily if it was tried to be removed,"},{"start":80.16,"end":86.36,"text":"and sign both the tape and the bag. That would be an effective double seal around the bottle."},{"start":86.36,"end":94.5,"text":"Some opinions considered putting the cork so tightly in the bottle that it would need a tool to remove it as one seal."},{"start":94.66,"end":99.62,"text":"And we also discussed the question of these new inventions of the caps with the combination lock."},{"start":99.76,"end":106.66,"text":"So if it only has a three-digit code, there are those who say it is not a good double seal because it's possible to crack within a half an hour."},{"start":106.66,"end":117.7,"text":"However, even those who are Machmir held that a four-digit code would be considered double seal because it would be very difficult for the non-Jew to crack the code."},{"start":118.44,"end":124.8,"text":"And again, a four-digit code would for sure be a double seal. A three-digit code, according to some, is a double seal. According to some, it's not."},{"start":125.34,"end":136.18,"text":"If a person, B'dievet, left a bottle with a non-Jew with only one seal, the Rama told us, for the Ashkenazim, the bottle is Muter B'dievet."},{"start":136.66,"end":145.88,"text":"And the Aruch HaShulchan said that because non-Jews don't lie bait nowadays, it's even enough to have one seal on a bottle."},{"start":145.98,"end":155.0,"text":"If a situation came up where a bottle was left with a non-Jew, but the circumstance is that the non-Jew knows that they are not allowed to touch the wine,"},{"start":155.1,"end":161.42,"text":"and on top of that they are afraid of getting caught touching the wine because they could lose their job, then the bottle would be permissible."},{"start":161.42,"end":181.18,"text":"We introduced also the solution of Yosef and Echnas. That's where we have the possibility for a Jew to show up at any time unexpectedly, and the non-Jew would be caught off guard if they were trying to touch the bottle and a Jew could come in at any time."},{"start":181.18,"end":195.78,"text":"So, in that situation, the non-Jew may be left with an open bottle of wine, again, assuming that they know that they're not allowed to touch it, and then they'd be afraid to do so because at any point a Jew can pop in without warning, that fear, that mirsos is enough to prevent them from doing so."},{"start":195.78,"end":200.26,"text":"However, there are four requirements that are necessary to rely on Yosef and Ichnas."},{"start":200.4,"end":204.44,"text":"The first one is that the non-Jew must think that the Jew can enter at any time."},{"start":204.96,"end":214.38,"text":"The second one is that when the Jew returns, the door must be unlocked, or at least if it's locked, they can see through the door clearly to the bottle of wine."},{"start":214.48,"end":220.2,"text":"The third requirement is that the non-Jew is not allowed to see the path that the Jew would take to get inside."},{"start":220.32,"end":224.74,"text":"If they can see it, we assume that they know, and they have ample warning to protect themselves."},{"start":239.38,"end":243.16,"text":"of wine. If a person was relying on Yetziv V'Nichas, and they"},{"start":243.16,"end":247.34,"text":"are lacking one of these requirements, and again, assuming that the non-Jew"},{"start":247.34,"end":251.34,"text":"is an idol worshiper or a Christian, or is a Christian, so then the bottle"},{"start":251.34,"end":255.18,"text":"and the barrel, if the bottle and barrel is open, so then the wine would be immediately"},{"start":255.18,"end":257.72,"text":"forbidden. We are concerned that they touched it and had"},{"start":258.72,"end":263.32,"text":"intent to libate. However, if the bottle or the barrel, which was left of the non-Jew, is closed"},{"start":263.32,"end":266.8,"text":"with a forgable seal, so then it's not forbidden immediately."},{"start":274.42,"end":289.84,"text":"If a person has live cameras pointed at the wine, and the non-Jew knows that they are not allowed to touch the wine, and they at least have a strong suspicion or know that you will be watching those cameras frequently, most posts can consider it a heter of Yetziv Adichnas, and it would protect the wine."},{"start":289.84,"end":297.52,"text":"wine. We lastly said that if a bottle of wine is hidden away in a place where it will not be able"},{"start":297.52,"end":303.12,"text":"to be found by a non-Jew, so then you could leave the non-Jew unsupervised in the house, and we are"},{"start":303.12,"end":308.7,"text":"not concerned that they have found the bottle and touched it as long as it is hidden very well."}]}];

